title: The Price Grid
shelf: quant
chapter: microstructure
subtopic: Tick Sizes
order: 1
summary: Prices can only sit on a grid of fixed steps called ticks. Tick size against tick value, and why professionals count profit and loss in ticks (Deltashark's futures example, 6 ticks on 4 contracts); large-tick and small-tick markets, and why the same 1-cent tick is huge for a $5 stock and tiny for a $500 one; what happens when the tick binds (the market makers' cost floor is below one tick, so the spread is stuck at one tick and the spare profit goes to whoever is at the front of the queue); what a place in the queue is worth, front against back; and the grid in FX (pips and half-pips), gold (10 cents an ounce) and bonds (32nds).
lede: 'Prices don''t move smoothly. Every market has a smallest step, the tick, and every bid and offer has to sit on that grid. It sounds like plumbing, but the size of the step decides how wide spreads can get, how long queues grow and what market makers compete on. This first note of the Microstructure chapter explains the grid and what a place in the queue is worth.'
