quiz:
  - q: 'A stock trades at $5 with a 1-cent tick. Market makers would happily quote a spread of 3 basis points (bp). What does the quoted spread look like?'
    opts: ['About 3 bp, because competition pushes it to the floor', 'It floats between 1 and 3 cents', 'Stuck at one tick, which is 20 bp. Makers can''t quote a better price, so they compete on getting to the front of the queue instead']
    right: 2
    why: 'One cent on a $5 stock is \(0.01 / 5 = 0.002\), or 20 bp, far above the 3 bp floor. The grid has no price between the bid and the offer, so the spread is pinned at one tick. The spare profit (20 bp against a 3 bp floor) goes to whoever is filled first, so the competition is over time and queue position, not price.'
  - q: 'A futures contract has a tick size of 0.25 points and a multiplier of 50. You make 4 ticks on 3 contracts. What is your profit?'
    opts: ['$150: the tick value is \(0.25 \times 50 = \$12.50\), and \(4 \times 12.50 \times 3 = 150\)', '$12.50', '$600']
    right: 0
    why: 'Tick value is tick size times the contract multiplier: \(0.25 \times 50 = \$12.50\) per contract. Profit is ticks times tick value times contracts: \(4 \times 12.50 \times 3 = \$150\). This is why desks count everything in ticks.'
  - q: 'You''re near the back of a long queue at the bid, and your order is filled. Why is that often bad news?'
    opts: ['It isn''t: a fill is always good for a market maker', 'Your order only gets reached when nearly the whole queue is eaten, which mostly happens when sellers are about to push the price through your level. So late fills tend to come just before the price drops', 'Because exchanges charge more for orders at the back']
    right: 1
    why: 'Small, ordinary selling only reaches the front of the queue. To reach the back, the selling has to be big enough to use up almost everything ahead of you, and that kind of selling usually moves the price down a tick. So the fills you get at the back are the ones you''d least want: adverse selection again.'

