{% sec 1, "A Ruler with Fixed Marks", "What is a tick, and why do traders count money in ticks?", "A ruler with fixed marks" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine a market stall where every price has to be a whole pound. Apples can be £1 or £2, but never £1.40. If the stallholder would really like to charge £1.40, they have a choice: charge £1 and lose money, or charge £2 and lose customers. And if two stalls both want to sell at "about £1.40", neither can undercut the other by a few pence. They both sit at £1 or £2, and whoever got there first gets served first.</p>
    <p>Every exchange works like that stall. Prices sit on a grid, and the smallest step on the grid is the {% gl "tick size" %}tick{% endgl %}. Deltashark's point is that the tick looks like plumbing, but it shapes spreads, queues and how market makers quote so strongly that changing it reorganises the whole market.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>{% gl "tick size" %}Tick size{% endgl %}<span class="why">The smallest price step: 1 cent for most US stocks, 0.25 index points for the E-mini S&amp;P 500 future.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>{% gl "tick value" %}Tick value{% endgl %}<span class="why">What one tick is worth in cash on one share or one contract: tick size × contract multiplier.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>The unit of P&amp;L<span class="why">Spreads, slippage and edge per fill are all a small number of ticks, so desks count in ticks.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Tick value, and profit in ticks</span>\[ \begin{gathered} \text{tick value} = \text{tick size} \times \text{multiplier} \\[6pt] \text{P\&L} = \text{ticks} \times \text{tick value} \times \text{contracts} \end{gathered} \]</div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark): ticks as the unit of P&amp;L</span>
    <h3>A futures contract has a tick size of \(0.25\) points and a multiplier of \(50\). A trade captures \(6\) ticks on \(4\) contracts. What does it earn?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Tick value: \(0.25 \times 50 = \$12.50\) per contract.</li>
      <li>Profit: \(6 \times 12.50 \times 4 = \$300\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">These are the E-mini S&amp;P 500's numbers. A trader on that desk would just say "six ticks on four lots". Every cost in the business, from the spread to slippage, is a few ticks, so the tick is the natural unit for everything in this chapter.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Market</th><th>Tick size</th><th>Tick value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US stocks (priced \(\$1\) or more)</td><td>\(\$0.01\)</td><td>\(\$0.01\) a share</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E-mini S&amp;P 500 future (CME)</td><td>\(0.25\) points</td><td>\(\$12.50\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gold future (COMEX, 100 ounces)</td><td>\(\$0.10\) an ounce</td><td>\(\$10\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10-year Treasury note future (CBOT)</td><td>half of \(\tfrac{1}{32}\) of a point</td><td>\(\$15.625\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2-year Treasury note future (CBOT)</td><td>an eighth of \(\tfrac{1}{32}\)</td><td>\(\$7.8125\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>EUR/USD spot</td><td>a pip, \(0.0001\) (or a fraction of one)</td><td>\(\$100\) per \(\text{€}1\) million per pip</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Big Ticks and Small Ticks", "Why does the same 1-cent tick matter more for some instruments than others?", "Big ticks and small ticks" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A 1-cent tick is the same for every US stock, but it isn't the same <em>size</em>. On a \(\$5\) stock, one cent is \(0.2\%\) of the price, or \(20\) {% gl "basis point" %}basis points{% endgl %}. On a \(\$500\) stock it's \(0.2\) bp, a hundred times smaller. What matters is how the tick compares with the spread market makers would quote if there were no grid at all, the {% gl "spread floor" %}floor{% endgl %} from Setting the Width. That splits instruments into two kinds, and Deltashark says they behave very differently.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>{% gl "large-tick" %}Large-tick{% endgl %}: the tick is bigger than the floor</th><th>{% gl "small-tick" %}Small-tick{% endgl %}: the tick is much smaller than the floor</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Quoted spread</td><td>Stuck at one tick almost all the time</td><td>Floats several ticks wide, moving with conditions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Queues at the best prices</td><td>Long and deep</td><td>Short</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What makers compete on</td><td><strong>Time</strong>: getting to the front of the queue</td><td><strong>Price</strong>: stepping ahead of each other by a tick</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quotes</td><td>Stable, the same prices for a long time</td><td>Flickering, as makers leapfrog each other by tiny amounts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Examples</td><td>Cheap stocks, the E-mini S&amp;P, Treasury futures</td><td>Expensive stocks, many smaller futures, crypto with very fine grids</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Researchers often define a large-tick asset as one whose spread is almost always exactly one tick (Dayri and Rosenbaum, 2012). Notice that it isn't only about price. The E-mini S&amp;P trades at thousands of points, so its \(0.25\) tick is a tiny fraction of a basis point. But it's so liquid that market makers would quote even tighter if they could, so it's the classic large-tick market, with queues of thousands of contracts at each price.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Large tick or small tick?</h3><p>Pick an instrument, or set your own price, tick and floor. The chart shows how often the quoted spread is 1, 2, 3 … ticks wide across a day of snapshots, if makers quote their floor rounded up to the grid.</p></div>
